light sleep stops the timer groups' watchdogs; ESP32 cache toggles are cheap

Two things ESP-IDF builds (6.0.2) found that Arduino builds do not:

- Light sleep, ESP32 and C3. ESP-IDF sleeps with interrupts off and the
  other CPU parked, so nothing feeds the interrupt watchdog (Timer Group
  1). On the chip that is fine: light sleep gates the clock the timer
  groups count, and their watchdogs stand still. Here they counted on, and
  any light sleep longer than the watchdog's timeout (300 ms by default)
  ended in an interrupt watchdog panic (C3) or a TG1 watchdog reset
  (ESP32), from the second sleep on. RTC_CNTL now raises a light-sleep
  line from SLEEP_EN until the wake-up (or a reset), wired to both timer
  groups, whose watchdogs keep their count and the time their stage had
  left while it is high. The general-purpose timers keep counting: ESP-IDF
  accounts for the sleep from RTC time either way. The S3 did not need it.

- ESP32 flash cache. ESP-IDF turns the cache off and on around every flash
  operation. The model unmapped and remapped the cache regions on each
  toggle, which rebuilt QEMU's memory map and flushed all translated code:
  a file system's small reads did that thousands of times a second, and
  SPIFFS_check() on a 1 MB partition did not finish in minutes (the C3 and
  S3 took 2 s). The regions now follow only the per-region masks, and
  turning the cache on still brings in the pages whose mapping changed
  (patch 0009's sync flushes code for exactly those). The check now takes
  about 7 s; the rest is the cost of parking the other CPU for each read.
  With the cache off a region still reads what it last held, where the
  chip would fault, which only firmware that is already broken notices.

Written for esp32bench (GPL-2.0-or-later). Changes Espressif's
hw/timer/esp32_timg.c, hw/timer/esp32c3_timg.c, hw/misc/esp32_rtc_cntl.c,
hw/misc/esp32c3_rtc_cntl.c, hw/misc/esp32_dport.c and their headers
(Copyright (c) 2019-2024 Espressif Systems (Shanghai) Co. Ltd.,
GPL-2.0-or-later, notices kept), and lcgamboa's hw/xtensa/esp32_picsimlab.c
and hw/riscv/esp32c3_picsimlab.c. Applied by esp32bench
(core/crates/sim/src/lcgamboa.rs) to lcgamboa/qemu at
885a6e7061fb70fac78a074a719423810bc19a17, after patch 0012.

diff --git a/hw/misc/esp32_dport.c b/hw/misc/esp32_dport.c
index f2c57d6..4ce4be9 100644
--- a/hw/misc/esp32_dport.c
+++ b/hw/misc/esp32_dport.c
@@ -289,20 +289,26 @@ static void esp32_cache_invalidate_all_entries(Esp32CacheRegionState* crs)
     }
 }
 
+/* esp32bench: ESP-IDF turns the cache off and on around every flash operation, so a
+ * file system's small reads toggle it thousands of times a second. Unmapping the
+ * regions each time rebuilt QEMU's memory map and flushed all translated code on
+ * every toggle: SPIFFS_check() on a 1 MB partition did not finish in minutes. The
+ * regions now follow only the per-region masks; turning the cache on still brings in
+ * the pages whose mapping changed (esp32_cache_data_sync flushes code for exactly
+ * those). With the cache off a region still reads what it last held, where the chip
+ * would fault, which only firmware that is already broken notices. */
 static void esp32_cache_state_update(Esp32CacheState* cs)
 {
     bool cache_enabled = FIELD_EX32(cs->cache_ctrl_reg, DPORT_PRO_CACHE_CTRL, CACHE_ENA) != 0;
 
-    bool drom0_enabled = cache_enabled &&
-        FIELD_EX32(cs->cache_ctrl1_reg, DPORT_PRO_CACHE_CTRL1, MASK_DROM0) == 0;
-    if (!cs->drom0.mem.enabled && drom0_enabled) {
+    bool drom0_enabled = FIELD_EX32(cs->cache_ctrl1_reg, DPORT_PRO_CACHE_CTRL1, MASK_DROM0) == 0;
+    if (cache_enabled && drom0_enabled) {
         esp32_cache_data_sync(&cs->drom0);
     }
     memory_region_set_enabled(&cs->drom0.mem, drom0_enabled);
 
-    bool iram0_enabled = cache_enabled &&
-        FIELD_EX32(cs->cache_ctrl1_reg, DPORT_PRO_CACHE_CTRL1, MASK_IRAM0) == 0;
-    if (!cs->iram0.mem.enabled && iram0_enabled) {
+    bool iram0_enabled = FIELD_EX32(cs->cache_ctrl1_reg, DPORT_PRO_CACHE_CTRL1, MASK_IRAM0) == 0;
+    if (cache_enabled && iram0_enabled) {
         esp32_cache_data_sync(&cs->iram0);
     }
     memory_region_set_enabled(&cs->iram0.mem, iram0_enabled);
